From: Alex Scotti <alexscotti@gmail.com>
Subject: vh265: a picture already output is never taken again as the first frame

output_pic() force-picks the first I picture for display in a Dolby Vision
dual-layer decoder (vdec->master or ->slave) whenever vf_pre_count is still
0 - without checking that the picture is waiting for output. When that
picture is recycled instead of shown (error_mark, nodisp_mark, drop_flag)
or prepare_display_buf() refuses it, vf_pre_count stays 0, and on a flush
output_pic_display()'s do { output_pic() } while (pic) takes the same
picture forever, in the decoder's threaded ISR.

On an AM9 Pro (S6) a Dolby Vision FEL enhancement-layer instance started
at the base layer's 3840x2160, changed to 1920x1088 on its first I frame,
and its flush looped: "VH265: output first frame poc 146" ~700,000 times,
irq/54 at 98% CPU. Closing the decoder then waits in vdec_free_irq() ->
synchronize_irq() for that thread, so Kodi's VideoPlayer thread sits in D
state, Kodi stops answering, and only a power cycle recovers the box. The
same storm ended a wedge on 2026-10-12.

Take only a picture with output_mark set, and bound output_pic_display()
at two passes per picture slot, logging if that bound is ever hit.

---
--- a/drivers/frame_provider/decoder/h265/vh265.c
+++ b/drivers/frame_provider/decoder/h265/vh265.c
@@ -4510,7 +4510,19 @@
 				continue;
 			if (pic->output_mark)
 				num_pic_not_yet_display++;
-			if (pic->slice_type == 2 &&
+			/*
+			 * Only a picture still waiting for output: one already
+			 * handed out or recycled (error/nodisp/drop, or refused by
+			 * prepare_display_buf) leaves vf_pre_count at 0, and taking
+			 * it again here made output_pic_display() loop forever in
+			 * the ISR thread on a flush - a Dolby Vision enhancement
+			 * layer started at the base layer's size, then its
+			 * resolution change ("output first frame poc 146" ~700k
+			 * times), with vdec_free_irq() waiting on it and the box
+			 * needing a power cycle.
+			 */
+			if (pic->output_mark &&
+				pic->slice_type == 2 &&
 				hevc->vf_pre_count == 0 &&
 				fast_output_enable & 0x1) {
 				/*fast output for first I picture*/
@@ -4594,8 +4606,18 @@
 void output_pic_display(struct hevc_state_s *hevc, unsigned char flush_flag, bool ones)
 {
 	struct PIC_s *pic_display = NULL;
+	/* each pass hands out or recycles one picture: more passes than
+	 * there are pictures is a picture handed out twice, so stop rather
+	 * than spin in the ISR thread */
+	int passes = 0;
 
 	do {
+		if (++passes > 2 * MAX_REF_PIC_NUM) {
+			hevc_print(hevc, 0,
+				"%s: output loop bound hit (poc %d) - stopping\n",
+				__func__, pic_display ? pic_display->POC : -1);
+			break;
+		}
 		pic_display = output_pic(hevc, flush_flag);
 
 		if (pic_display) {
